# 1. Purpose

The Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server is the core component of the Commerce Operations Copilot.

Instead of allowing an AI model to directly interact with backend systems or databases, the MCP Server exposes a set of business-oriented tools that represent real operational capabilities.

The MCP Server is responsible for:

- Exposing business capabilities
- Validating tool requests
- Delegating work to business services
- Returning structured responses
- Providing a safe interface between the LLM and the commerce backend

It is intentionally designed as the operational layer of the application rather than a thin wrapper around backend APIs.

---

# 2. Design Principles

The MCP Server follows these principles:

- Business-first tool design
- Thin tool handlers
- Structured inputs and outputs
- Deterministic execution
- Safe action execution
- Framework-independent implementation

Each tool represents a complete business capability instead of a low-level database operation.

---

# 3. MCP Architecture

```text
User

↓

Claude / ChatGPT

↓

Hosted MCP Server

↓

Tool

↓

Business Service

↓

Repository

↓

Prisma

↓

SQLite
```

The MCP Server is the only entry point used by AI clients.

---

# 4. Available Tools

The server exposes four tools.

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| investigate_order | Diagnose why an order cannot proceed through fulfillment |
| execute_resolution | Execute a predefined operational resolution |
| get_order_timeline | Display the lifecycle events of an order |
| find_orders_needing_attention | Return orders requiring operational review |

Each tool is designed around an operational task rather than CRUD functionality.

---

# 5. Tool Specifications

## 5.1 investigate_order

### Description

Investigates an order across payment, inventory, and shipment systems to determine the primary reason it is blocked.

### Input

```json
{
  "orderId": "ORD-102"
}
```

### Output

```json
{
  "orderId": "ORD-102",
  "status": "BLOCKED",
  "rootCause": "Payment Failed",
  "confidence": 0.96,
  "evidence": [
    "Payment authorization failed",
    "Inventory available",
    "Shipment not created"
  ],
  "recommendedAction": "retry_payment"
}
```

---

## 5.2 execute_resolution

### Description

Executes a predefined operational action for an order.

### Supported Actions

- retry_payment
- release_inventory
- recreate_shipment

### Input

```json
{
  "orderId": "ORD-102",
  "action": "retry_payment"
}
```

### Output

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Payment retry completed.",
  "updatedStatus": "READY_FOR_FULFILLMENT"
}
```

---

## 5.3 get_order_timeline

### Description

Returns the important lifecycle events associated with an order.

### Input

```json
{
  "orderId": "ORD-102"
}
```

### Output

```json
{
  "orderId": "ORD-102",
  "timeline": [
    {
      "time": "2026-07-25T10:00:00Z",
      "event": "Order Created"
    },
    {
      "time": "2026-07-25T10:02:00Z",
      "event": "Payment Failed"
    },
    {
      "time": "2026-07-25T10:04:00Z",
      "event": "Inventory Reserved"
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## 5.4 find_orders_needing_attention

### Description

Returns orders that require immediate operational review.

### Example Response

```json
[
  {
    "orderId": "ORD-102",
    "priority": "HIGH",
    "reason": "Payment Failed"
  },
  {
    "orderId": "ORD-108",
    "priority": "MEDIUM",
    "reason": "Shipment Delayed"
  }
]
```

